# PR #1382 review comments — cdz-kernel/src/kernel.rs (v-agent-harness)

Mirrored from https://github.com/camshaft/cadenza/pull/1382 (PR: "[v-agent-harness] 3b6a5d081").
This is the deliver_async migration that unblocks the #1351/#1358 async chain.

## 1. `deliver_async` doc has stale-prone forward-looking migration notes (Copilot, kernel.rs:339) — doc
> The `deliver_async` doc comment contains forward-looking migration notes and concrete
> implementation specifics (e.g. named future executors / "removed once every caller…") that are
> likely to become stale. Consider rewriting this as a present-tense API contract focusing on
> observable behavior: it appends the inbound event and drives the loop to quiescence, awaiting only
> reducer folding via `AsyncReducer`.

## 2. `fire_due_timers_async` (new public API) has no test coverage (Copilot, kernel.rs:356) — test-coverage
> `fire_due_timers_async` is a new public API but there is no test coverage asserting its behavior
> (deadline-order firing, `fired_ms` equals the timer's recorded deadline, and that firing settles
> the timer/open-obligation set). Adding an async test would help prevent the sync/async timer paths
> from drifting.

## 3. "async == sync delivery" test never compares against the sync path (Copilot, kernel.rs:1505) — test-rigor
> This test's name/comment say it proves async delivery is identical to sync delivery, but it
> currently only asserts a single expected snapshot and never compares against the sync path.
> Consider creating a sync baseline session and asserting the two snapshots match; that makes the
> equivalence claim precise and guards against drift between `deliver` and `deliver_async`.

Points 2+3 are the substantive ones: the WHOLE safety property of this async migration is that
deliver_async / fire_due_timers_async behave identically to their sync counterparts, but neither is
actually pinned against the sync path. Build a sync baseline session and assert snapshot-equality
(and add a fire_due_timers_async behavior test) so a future drift between the two paths fails loudly.
Point 1 is the standard "present-tense contract, not migration notes" doc reword.
